# Copy to .env and adjust for local dev if you don't want the defaults.
# Railway injects these directly per-environment (staging/production) —
# this file is just the reference list, not loaded automatically by the Go
# binary (no dotenv dependency; keep config as real env vars, 12-factor style).

# Port the API listens on.
# dev: 8080 (default if unset) · staging/production: set by Railway
PORT=8080

# Comma-separated origins allowed to call this API cross-origin (CORS).
# Only relevant to browser-based clients (apps/web, the Expo web preview) —
# native iOS/Android requests aren't subject to CORS at all.
# dev: http://localhost:3000 (apps/web), http://localhost:8081 (Expo web preview)
# staging: https://staging.yourdomain.com (placeholder, not registered yet)
# production: https://yourdomain.com (placeholder, not registered yet)
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:8081

# Clerk instance issuer URL — required, no default. Used to fetch the JWKS
# for verifying session tokens (no Clerk secret key needed for this; that's
# only for privileged Backend API calls we don't make yet). Find it by
# decoding your NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY (base64 middle segment) or
# from the Clerk dashboard's API Keys page ("Frontend API URL").
CLERK_ISSUER=https://your-instance.clerk.accounts.dev

# N134/#538: azp (authorized-party) validation. A Clerk session token's
# `azp` claim names the origin the token was requested from (set from the
# request's `Origin` header at issuance — see Clerk's session-tokens docs).
# Once more than one frontend shares a Clerk instance, checking it rules out
# a token minted for a different app being replayed against this API.
#
# CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES is the allowlist: comma-separated origins/parties
# permitted to call this API, same convention as WEB_ORIGIN and
# ADMIN_USER_IDS above. e.g. https://app.vola.example,https://admin.vola.example
#
# Unset/empty is a SUPPORTED state — azp checking is then a no-op, which is
# the local-dev default (nobody sets this on a laptop, and it shouldn't
# suddenly become required there). See auth.Verifier.authorizedParty in
# internal/platform/auth/auth.go for the full per-token policy, including
# the documented (assumption, not independently verified against a live
# mobile token) decision to ALLOW a token with no azp claim at all — Clerk
# omits `azp` when there's no Origin header to capture, which native mobile
# requests (apps/mobile) don't send.
CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES=

# Set to exactly "true" to make this deployment's boot FAIL if
# CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES above is empty, rather than silently running with
# azp checking disabled. Opt-in and separate from the allowlist itself on
# purpose: this project has no other "local dev vs. real deployment" switch
# baked into the binary (Railway just injects different values per
# environment), so this must not turn on implicitly the moment somebody
# pulls a change — only when a deployment's own trust model actually
# requires the allowlist to be present. Set this alongside
# CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES once staging/production have one, so an
# accidental future unset of the allowlist fails the deploy loudly instead
# of quietly re-opening this gap.
CLERK_REQUIRE_AZP=

# Postgres connection string.
# dev: matches docker-compose.yml (run `docker compose up -d` first)
# staging/production: Railway injects its own managed Postgres's DATABASE_URL
DATABASE_URL=postgres://vola:vola_dev_only@localhost:5432/vola?sslmode=disable

# Postgres connection string for the `*_test.go` integration tests. They
# skip silently when this is unset — which meant that for a long time they
# only ever really ran in CI, and `go test ./...` passing locally proved
# less than it appeared to. Point it at a *separate* database from
# DATABASE_URL so a test run can never touch your dev data:
#   docker compose exec postgres createdb -U vola vola_test
#   cd backend && DATABASE_URL=<the vola_test URL> go run ./cmd/migrate up
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://vola:vola_dev_only@localhost:5432/vola_test?sslmode=disable

# Comma-separated Clerk user IDs (the JWT's `sub` claim) allowed to call
# admin-only endpoints (GET /v1/admin/*). Same convention apps/admin uses
# for its own frontend gate — one admin allowlist, not two. Empty/unset
# means nobody can reach admin endpoints (fails closed).
ADMIN_USER_IDS=user_your_clerk_user_id_here

# Public origin the exercise catalog's media is served from — an R2 custom
# domain in production (e.g. https://media.vola.app), or the r2.dev
# development URL Cloudflare generates when you enable public access.
#
# Only the storage *key* is stored per row; the API joins it onto this at
# read time. That keeps the bucket and CDN hostname out of the database, so
# moving either is an env-var change rather than a data migration.
#
# Unset is a supported state, not a misconfiguration: local dev and CI have
# no bucket, and media then reports an empty URL rather than a broken one.
MEDIA_BASE_URL=

# Private object storage for body check-in photos — an R2 bucket, reached
# through its S3-compatible API.
#
# SEPARATE FROM MEDIA_BASE_URL ABOVE, and the separation is the point. The
# exercise catalog's media is identical for everybody and is served public and
# CDN-cacheable. A progress photo is the most sensitive thing this product
# stores: it is written and read only through short-lived presigned URLs, the
# bytes never pass through this API, and no public origin can reach it.
#
# Create an R2 API token with Object Read & Write scoped to the bucket; that
# gives the access key id and secret. The endpoint is
# https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com — the S3 API host, NOT the
# public r2.dev URL.
#
# All four unset is a SUPPORTED state: the photo endpoints report 503 and the
# rest of the check-in works. Setting only some of them is fatal at startup —
# a half-configured bucket silently loses photos, which is worse than not
# starting.
R2_ENDPOINT=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# A successful request slower than this is recorded as a symptom in
# `health_events` and shown on the admin health screen. Defaults to 2000.
# Environment-specific on purpose: a shared Railway instance and a laptop with
# a local Postgres do not agree on what "slow" means.
SLOW_REQUEST_MS=2000

# Meal estimation (POST /v1/nutrition/estimate). Optional: without a key for the
# selected provider, that one route serves 503 and every other nutrition route
# runs normally.
#
# WHICH MODEL DRAFTS A MEAL IS CONFIG, NOT CODE — trying a different one is an
# env change and a restart. Leave both unset for the default, which is the
# cheapest model measured to do this job well.
#
# The key is read from the variable the SELECTED provider names, so set the one
# that matches ESTIMATE_PROVIDER. Setting the other has no effect and looks
# exactly like an outage (503 on that route only).
#
# CHANGING THE PROVIDER HAS A SECOND HALF. apps/mobile/app/food/describe.tsx
# tells the athlete which company their photograph is sent to, BEFORE the
# camera opens, and it is hardcoded — the response's `model` field arrives long
# after the picture has left the phone. Point this at a different provider
# without changing that string and the app makes a specific false statement
# about where a photo of somebody's kitchen went. A Go test pins the default
# against it; an env var set in a deployment is outside what that test can see.
#
#   ESTIMATE_PROVIDER   openai (default) | anthropic
#   ESTIMATE_MODEL      overrides the provider's default model id
#                       openai defaults to gpt-5.6-luna
#                       anthropic defaults to claude-haiku-4-5
ESTIMATE_PROVIDER=
ESTIMATE_MODEL=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Dictated BJJ reflections (POST /v1/bjj/reflect/draft). Optional, same shape as
# the meal estimator above: without a key for the selected provider, that one
# route serves 503 and every other bjj route runs normally.
#
# ITS OWN PROVIDER AND MODEL VARS, not the estimate's. Model tier is a
# per-feature judgement, and this is the one feature whose tier was chosen
# against a published measurement: N37 ran the whole dictation corpus through
# both OpenAI tiers and got 0.0% invention on gpt-5.6-luna against 24.2% on
# gpt-5.4-nano, on the same prompt. Nano's inventions are all the same mistake —
# resolving a phrase the athlete did not narrow — and a validator cannot catch
# it, because the id it invents is a real one. Do not point this at the cheap
# tier to save money; see evals/bjj-dictation/README.md before moving it at all.
#
# THE DICTATION LEAVES THE DEVICE. Transcription happens on the phone's own
# keyboard, so no audio ever reaches this API, but the sentence does — it is
# forwarded to the provider named here and discarded, stored nowhere and never
# logged. A client has to tell the athlete which company that is before they
# dictate, so pointing this at a different provider has a second half in the
# client, exactly as the estimator's does.
#
#   REFLECT_PROVIDER   openai (default) | anthropic
#   REFLECT_MODEL      overrides the provider's default model id
#                      openai defaults to gpt-5.6-luna (measured)
#                      anthropic defaults to claude-haiku-4-5 (NOT measured on
#                      this task — it is there so a key swap works, not as a
#                      claim that it scores the same)
REFLECT_PROVIDER=
REFLECT_MODEL=
